  "description": "Two diagnostics on the nucleon-ordering degree of freedom (bean qou-48is, loose ends of PR #4851). (1) hecke_core_symbolic._ground_state_ordering returns `pnp` for 3He while prop:ground-state-ordering rule (iii) says sorted `ppn` wins for A <= 3. Computing the proposition's own objective Phi_q = sum_i log|V_i| over EVERY arrangement at the experimental proton anchor shows the argmin at (Z,N)=(2,1) is `ppn`: the PROPOSITION is right and the HELPER is wrong. The helper performs no variational search (it is a fixed interleaving generator identical to _alternating_ordering_sym), is not the Phi_q argmin across an exhaustive A <= 14 sweep, and is not the extremum of any other objective the corpus states -- so the disagreement is a defect, not a definitional difference. The fix is NOT applied: it moves atom_V_full_sym / build_atom_nf_sym and hence published witness values, which is outside this session's scope bar. (2) 3He (2,1) and 4He (2,2) are absent from mass-table-comprehensive.witness.json because of a dedup guard `if a > 4:  # Avoid duplicates with explicitly added H, He isotopes` whose premise is false -- the explicit ISOTOPES list contains only H-chain entries (p, n, D, T, all Z <= 1), so no Z >= 2 grid row can duplicate it. The guard drops exactly 6 unique (Z, N) rows, of which 3He and 4He are the only bound nuclides. free_parameters = 0; no AME/CODATA quantity selects anything; nothing is wired; no published number changed. Sharpening: on the BARE NUCLEON chain the two 3He arrangements are EXACTLY degenerate -- the transfer factors T(d) = S + d.1 all commute, so the readout depends on the crossing coefficients only as a multiset (proved sorry-free over a generic CommRing in QOU/MassTheory/TransferFactorCommute.lean). Rule (iii)'s 'sorted BEATS interleaving' is thus a TIE at 3He, strict only after the electron tail is appended -- and that separation is produced by the UNDEFINED (n, e) crossing coefficient, which _phi1_from_ordering_sym silently skips. Both readings put sorted at the optimum; neither supports the helper's pnp.",
